nefit  from it who has gotten their shot  assuming they both have similar risk  statuses and  are in similar age groups which one of  those two people would be prioritized in  uh in  alberta well dr dr insha that's a  medical question so over to you  the current eligibility criteria include  all those who are either immunized or  unimmunized who have a significant  immune compromising condition such as  somebody who's under treatment for  cancer  someone who's taking medication for an  autoimmune condition that's suppressing  their immune system it also includes  those who don't have the protection of  vaccines who have other conditions  such as diabetes for example  what the protocol is is that as  individuals become identified as having  covid19  they're referred through the system to  determine if they meet the eligibility  criteria they then have a conversation  with a clinician who can determine based  on that particular individual's needs  and  clinical conditions  and their context which of the two  treatment options so we do have paxlovid  we also have cetrova satrovamab the  monoclonal antibody treatment that we've  had available for several months  and which of those two treatments would  be recommended for that individual  there is at this time of course a  limited supply but as people become  eligible they will be offered that  treatment  and if they fall into any of those  eligibility criteria the  that treatment will be offered as long  as it is available and hopefully we will  be able to access more supply in the  coming weeks and months what we do know  again is that we have both cetrovamab  and paxlovid  and so far in terms of the demand for  cetrovamab we have had sufficient to  meet the demand for people who qualify  so it would be my hope that that same  would be true now that we have this  additional medication to add to  that  suite of options for early treatment  but i would of course always encourage  those who have not yet received the  bene