h right now and also if you  just clarify that and also with all the  you know our our death count seems to be  just the numbers seem so much higher now  each day than they were before is that  really indicative of a of a pandemic  that's kind of slowing down  well first of all with respect to every  death of course is a terrible sadness uh  and uh my condolences go out to all of  those who who have passed away from  covet and from all other  health conditions over uh the past two  years and everybody else impacted  indirectly in the healthcare system  through post moments of surgeries and  and all of this has been  incredibly difficult and in some cases  very tragic for families  we can continue to expect to see  regrettably hospitalizations and  fatalities as a result of this virus  and uh when you're dealing with such  large numbers as we are  probably hundreds of thousands of  albertans who currently have active  infections millions of canadians  um and even though the percentages of  those infected  who  um  pass away from  omicron are much lower than the  percentages who have  died from earlier variants of covet 19  it's still  uh unfortunately  going to affect some people uh in with  the most severe outcomes both  hospitalizations and fatalities  uh in terms of the the numbers what i'm  telling you is that our total overall  inpatient  beds  uh number of inpatients i should say in  our  100 hospital network has been fairly  constant through the month of january  averaging between  200 and 5 300 well we have about  6 400 beds available so system wide  we're operating at 87  of capacity  and that is uh lower than is typically  the case uh in pre-covered years  uh we have seen  um  at the peak for we've seen higher  numbers often uh overall in the in the  top 16 hospitals system capacity often  peaking out in the high 90s so look i'm  not for a moment diminishing the stress  that the hospitals are under  it is real it's added to by the  complexity of caring for 